= Dissent Within the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span>

Despite [+ royalist_leader +] forcing <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span> commander Hadrian Tessen to sign a statement of submission, the renegade has bolted once again. Proclaiming defiance against his state steward, he’s formed an independent paramilitary group—taking some 7,000 loyal <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span> men with him. [+ royalist_leader +] retaliated by expelling him outright from the party.

In past cases, condemnation from [+ royalist_leader +] was enough to snuff out dissent. But this time, Tessen defied the ban and retained fierce loyalty among his ranks. Local <span style="color: #7A3C00;">Royalist</span> organizations are now split—forced to choose between backing their steward or Tessen—crippling their state organization.

- @nazi_2
- @exploit: Court the disillusioned rank and file.

@nazi_2
title: Is this the start of something greater?
on-arrival: loyalist_strength -= 75; loyalist_militancy -= 0.1; vanguard_strength += 30; disfavored_royalist -= 4; disfavored_collectivist += 2; royalist_explode += 1

Although Tessen is careful not to denounce [+ royalist_leader +] directly, it is clear that his break stems from deep dissatisfaction with the party’s current strategy. He's called for a more violent seizure of power, arguing that the "movement’s historic moment has been missed", and that instead of clinging to the "mania of legality", they must act "in a more brutal and revolutionary fashion".

Tessen’s appeals to fellow <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span> leaders across the country have met with favorable responses in several regions. In Stathmore, many <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span> men defected or were expelled en masse, forming their own paramilitary group that seized local headquarters and shut down party-run hostels and soup kitchens on claims of misappropriation.

In Axton, Crowde loyalists wait in vain for a signal from the man they once believed would challenge [+ royalist_leader +], but none comes. Even so, small-scale defections and expulsions continue to spread across the country.

Although this crisis has weakened the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Loyalists**</span>, many of its frustrated members have instead channeled their fury into increasingly frequent and bloody street battles—against not just the Banners, but the <span style="color: #7A3C00;">**Royalists**</span>'s own Crownguard as well.

While discontent remains aimed at local party leaders, shielding [+ royalist_leader +] for now, it's only a matter of time before the façade crumbles.

@exploit
on-arrival: loyalist_strength -= 100; loyalist_militancy -= 0.1; vanguard_strength += 30; disfavored_royalist -= 5; disfavored_collectivist += 2; royalist_explode += 1; commons_marcher += 2; disfavored_marcher += 2; commons_royalist -= 2

The crisis is the same — Tessen's ban defied, the regional commands wavering — but we decline to watch it from the gallery. Party canvassers move quietly through the Loyalist strongholds with a simple message for the disillusioned: the movement that promised them bread has fed them parades. Not many cross over; more simply go home. Every man who hangs up the brown coat, whichever way he goes, is one the Sovereigntists must replace.